Then any loop that might run long does begin break? if exit then ... again. It's cooperative — a begin again with no break? still hangs. That's the Forth discipline and it's the right trade for a toy: no preemption, no timer interrupt, no longjmp, no state to save. Document it once at the top of frac.frac.

Gap 1: persistence — the real one

Three levels, increasing complexity. Do them in order.

Level 1 — in-session RAM store (half a day, ~80 lines)

Named buffers in RAM. Survives within a session but not across reboot.

```c
#define MAXFILES 16
typedef struct { char name[24]; char data[4096]; int len; } KFile;
static KFile g_files[MAXFILES];
static int g_nfiles = 0;

HELPER void h_save(void) {
    if (!need(2)) return;
    Rat len = *--g_sp; Rat addr = *--g_sp;
    if (len.num > 4095) return;
    // name comes next on stack; convention: "name" "data" save → (addr,len) popped, then name
    if (!need(2)) return;
    Rat nlen = *--g_sp; Rat naddr = *--g_sp;
    if (nlen.num > 23) return;
    char name[24]; memcpy(name, (char*)naddr.num, nlen.num); name[nlen.num] = 0;
    KFile* f = NULL;
    for (int i = 0; i < g_nfiles; i++) if (!strcmp(g_files[i].name, name)) { f = &g_files[i]; break; }
    if (!f) { if (g_nfiles >= MAXFILES) return; f = &g_files[g_nfiles++]; }
    strncpy(f->name, name, 24);
    memcpy(f->data, (char*)addr.num, len.num);
    f->len = len.num;
}
HELPER void h_load(void) {
    // similar: pop (name_addr, name_len), find file, feed_source(f->data)
}
HELPER void h_files(void) {
    for (int i = 0; i < g_nfiles; i++) { kputs(g_files[i].name); kputc('\n'); }
}
```

Usage:

```
Frac> "foo.frac" "def sq dup * ;" save
Frac> "foo.frac" load
Frac> 7 sq
49
```

This is genuinely enough for interactive development. It doesn't survive reboot, but it lets you iterate on a program in a session without retyping. That's the actual workflow you want.

Level 2 — disk persistence via ATA PIO (~200 lines, a weekend)

The boot device (your USB or -drive in QEMU) is ATA drive 0. Reserve sectors 100–355 for a 128 KB file store. ATA PIO write is a sequence of outb/inb to ports 0x1F0–0x1F7; the sequence is documented in the ATA spec and every hobby OS tutorial.

The format is trivial:

```
sector 100:  header { "FRACFS01", uint32 file_count }
sector 101+: directory { name[24], start_sector, sector_count } × N
sector 200+: file data
```

Then save/load/files do PIO reads/writes instead of touching the RAM store. This survives reboot. One caveat: USB devices only respond to ATA PIO if the BIOS presented them as legacy ATA. On QEMU -drive format=raw they do. On real hardware via USB they usually don't — you'd need a USB mass-storage driver or an XHCI stack, which is a month. For a toy, target QEMU for the persistence demo and note that real-hardware persistence is the one thing that requires more work.

Level 3 — real filesystem (skip)

FAT12, ext2, a custom FS with directories and metadata. Not on the critical path. The sector-store from Level 2 is enough for the self-hosted compiler to read frac.frac and lib/*.frac.

What I'd do, in order

1. key? / key / break? — an afternoon. Now interactive programs work.
2. main convention in the REPL — 5 lines. Now load foo.frac runs foo's main.
3. RAM file store (save / load / files) — a half-day. Now you can iterate on a program within a session.
4. Boot into Level 2 (ATA PIO persistence) — a weekend. Now programs survive reboot, and the self-hosted compiler at Stage 2 has a place to read from.

After step 4, the OS has everything the self-hosting arc needs: a place to save source, a way to load it, a way to run it, and a way to stop it. Every gap remaining after that is either a library (more graphics, more math) or a compiler feature (locals, error reporting) — none of them structural.

