calc.frac — a self-editing calculator, in Frac, on FracOS

This is the capstone, and it's cheaper than it looks because the compiler you already have does the evaluating. The calculator doesn't need its own parser — it's a line editor on top of eval. Two things are missing: an eval primitive, and the UI. That's it.

The missing primitive: eval

Nothing else in the language can say "compile this string and run it." That's what makes calc.frac impossible today. It's 30 lines of C:

```c
HELPER void h_eval(void) {
    if (!need(2)) return;
    Rat len = *--g_sp; Rat addr = *--g_sp;
    if (len.num > 1000) return;
    char buf[1024];
    memcpy(buf, (char*)addr.num, (size_t)len.num);
    buf[len.num] = 0;

    static char tk[256][64];
    int ntok = tokenize(buf, tk, 256);
    if (!ntok) return;

    // save every compiler global — def bodies in the user input will have
    // committed to g_exec_used, which is correct and persistent
    size_t s_ls = g_line_start, s_pos = g_pos;
    int s_if = g_if_sp, s_beg = g_begin_sp, s_wh = g_while_sp;

    g_line_start = g_exec_used; g_pos = 0;
    g_if_sp = 0; g_begin_sp = 0; g_while_sp = 0;
    compile_tokens(tk, ntok);
    if (g_pos > 0) {
        emit_ret();
        size_t cs = g_line_start;
        g_exec_used = g_line_start + g_pos;
        ((void(*)(void))(g_exec + cs))();
    }
    g_line_start = s_ls; g_pos = s_pos;
    g_if_sp = s_if; g_begin_sp = s_beg; g_while_sp = s_wh;
}
```

Add add_word("eval", h_eval); to bootstrap(). This is what makes Frac self-interpreting — every later trick depends on it. It also answers the self-hosting question directly: at Stage 2, the self-hosted compiler is what eval becomes when it's written in Frac.

