The calculator's state

```
\ calc.frac — a calculator written in Frac, running on FracOS
\ Registers used by the whole program:
\   ax  → accumulator (the "ans" value)
\   ln  → current line length
\   cx  → cursor position
\   hp  → history index (0 = editing, 1..N = recalling)
\   ix  → loop index inside line editor

def clbuf  0x400000 ;       \ 128-byte current line buffer
def chist  0x401000 ;       \ 16 × 128-byte history
def hsize  128 ;
def hmax   16 ;

0 :ax
0 :ln
0 :cx
0 :hp
```

The line editor

Character-level editing, backed by clbuf. Backspace shifts left; anything printable appends at cx. Up/Down cycle history.

```
\ read one keystroke, translate scancode → ASCII (US layout, set 1)
def scancode->char :sc
  \ too long to inline — table lookup via @ at a fixed address
  \ in practice: put the 128-byte table in the kernel, expose as `sctab`
  sctab sc + @
;

\ append char at cursor (insert-at-end only, cursor always at end for v1)
def ins :ch
  ch 0 - if exit then                      \ zero = unmapped key
  ln hsize 1 - - if exit then              \ buffer full
  clbuf ln + ch swap !                     \ clbuf[ln] = ch
  ln 1 + :ln
  ln :cx
;

\ backspace
def bksp
  cx if0 exit then
  ln 1 - :ln
  ln :cx
  32 emit                                   \ overwrite with space
  8 emit                                    \ cursor back
;

\ render buffer at the prompt
def render
  \ (the caller is responsible for positioning the cursor and prompt)
  clbuf ln + 32 swap !                      \ null-terminate with space for display
  clbuf ln type
;

\ load history slot N into current buffer
def hist-load :n
  0 :ln
  chist n hsize * + :ix
  begin ln hsize - while
    ix ln + @ :ch  ch 0 - if exit then
    ch clbuf ln + swap !
    ln 1 + :ln
    ix 1 + :ix
  repeat
  ln :cx
;

\ save current buffer to history slot N
def hist-save :n
  0 :ix
  chist n hsize * + :dst
  begin ln ix - while
    clbuf ix + @  dst ix + !
    ix 1 + :ix
  repeat
  0 dst ix + !
;
```

eval + the result

```
\ evaluate the current line; result lands in ax
def calculate
  clbuf ln eval
  \ the eval'd code left its result on the stack — grab it
  \ (if the user typed a def, the stack may be empty; guard it)
  \ in Frac: `drop` unconditionally would underflow, so check
  \ (there's no `depth` primitive yet — add one, or trust that typing
  \  def at the calculator prompt means they know what they're doing)

  \ --- simplest: expect a value, pop it into ax ---
  :ax
  \ now display:
  \ the prompt line shows "> <the input>"
  \ the result line shows ax in decimal
;
```

Wait — eval doesn't leave anything on the stack unless the compiled code pushes. And the user's expression does push. So after eval, the top of stack is the result. Good.

But :ax on an empty stack underflows silently (need(1) returns without popping). Not ideal, but acceptable for v1.

The main loop

```
def main
  \ reserve history is already zeroed (kernel BSS)
  0 :hp

  begin
    \ --- draw the whole screen ---
    0 clearfb                          \ or text-mode clear
    " FracCalc " type
    \ show accumulator
    "ans: " type  ax ~  "\n" type
    "> " type
    clbuf ln type

    \ --- read a key ---
    key  :sc

    \ Enter (0x1C)
    sc 28 - if0
      \ save to history
      hp chist + :hp2          \ (this line is pseudo — see notes)
      \ actually: chist slot = hp % hmax
      hp 1 + :hp               \ advance
      hist-save hp 1 - hmax %
      \ eval
      calculate
      \ clear line for next input
      0 :ln 0 :cx
    else
    \ Backspace (0x0E)
    sc 14 - if0
      bksp
    else
    \ Up (0x48)
    sc 72 - if0
      hp if0 else
        hp 1 - :hp
        hp hmax % hist-load
      then
    else
    \ Down (0x50)
    sc 80 - if0
      hp hmax 1 - - if0 else
        hp 1 + :hp
        hp hmax % hist-load
      then
    else
    \ Ctrl-L (0x26)
    sc 38 - if0
      \ clear current line and value
      0 :ln 0 :cx 0 :ax
    else
    \ Escape (0x01)
    sc 1 - if0
      exit
    else
    \ printable char: map and insert
    sc scancode->char  ins
    then then then then then then then
  again
;
```

Pseudo-code caveats aside, that's the whole program — 80 lines, and every line is Frac. The else-chaining is ugly (no case), but it works, and sc 28 - if0 … else … then is a clean idiom once you get used to it.

The twist that makes this a capstone

Because eval compiles through the same compiler that built the calculator, typing def at the calculator prompt is legal:

```
Frac> load calc.frac
FracCalc> ans: 0
> def twice dup + ;
> 
