        g_exec_used = g_line_start + g_pos;
        ((void(*)(void))(g_exec + cs))();
    }
    g_line_start = s_ls; g_pos = s_pos;
    g_if_sp = s_if; g_begin_sp = s_beg; g_while_sp = s_wh;
}
```

Add add_word("eval", h_eval); to bootstrap(). This is what makes Frac self-interpreting — every later trick depends on it. It also answers the self-hosting question directly: at Stage 2, the self-hosted compiler is what eval becomes when it's written in Frac.

Reserve two buffers in the kernel:

```c
static uint8_t g_calc_line[128];                // current input
static uint8_t g_calc_hist[16][128];            // 16 lines × 128 bytes
add_word("clbuf", ...);   // actually — expose via string constants:
```

Simplest: define clbuf and chist as words that push their addresses.

