add_word("chist", (void*)(uintptr_t)g_calc_hist);
```

Then clbuf pushes the buffer address as a Rat (via a tiny helper h_push_addr, or reuse helper + a small wrapper). Actually simpler — define them as def clbuf 0x400000 ; constants in calc.frac and have the kernel reserve those physical addresses. Since we identity-mapped everything, 0x400000 is writable and executable. Do that.

The calculator's state

```
\ calc.frac — a calculator written in Frac, running on FracOS
\ Registers used by the whole program:
\   ax  → accumulator (the "ans" value)
\   ln  → current line length
\   cx  → cursor position
\   hp  → history index (0 = editing, 1..N = recalling)
\   ix  → loop index inside line editor

def clbuf  0x400000 ;       \ 128-byte current line buffer
def chist  0x401000 ;       \ 16 × 128-byte history
def hsize  128 ;
def hmax   16 ;

0 :ax
0 :ln
0 :cx
0 :hp
```

The line editor

Character-level editing, backed by clbuf. Backspace shifts left; anything printable appends at cx. Up/Down cycle history.

```
\ read one keystroke, translate scancode → ASCII (US layout, set 1)
def scancode->char :sc
  \ too long to inline — table lookup via @ at a fixed address
  \ in practice: put the 128-byte table in the kernel, expose as `sctab`
  sctab sc + @
;

\ append char at cursor (insert-at-end only, cursor always at end for v1)
def ins :ch
  ch 0 - if exit then                      \ zero = unmapped key
  ln hsize 1 - - if exit then              \ buffer full
  clbuf ln + ch swap !                     \ clbuf[ln] = ch
  ln 1 + :ln
  ln :cx
;

\ backspace
def bksp
  cx if0 exit then
  ln 1 - :ln
  ln :cx
  32 emit                                   \ overwrite with space
  8 emit                                    \ cursor back
;

\ render buffer at the prompt
def render
  \ (the caller is responsible for positioning the cursor and prompt)
  clbuf ln + 32 swap !                      \ null-terminate with space for display
  clbuf ln type
;

\ load history slot N into current buffer
def hist-load :n
  0 :ln
  chist n hsize * + :ix
  begin ln hsize - while
    ix ln + @ :ch  ch 0 - if exit then
    ch clbuf ln + swap !
    ln 1 + :ln
    ix 1 + :ix
  repeat
  ln :cx
;

\ save current buffer to history slot N
def hist-save :n
  0 :ix
  chist n hsize * + :dst
  begin ln ix - while
    clbuf ix + @  dst ix + !
    ix 1 + :ix
  repeat
  0 dst ix + !
;
```

eval + the result

```
\ evaluate the current line; result lands in ax
def calculate
  clbuf ln eval
  \ the eval'd code left its result on the stack — grab it
  \ (if the user typed a def, the stack may be empty; guard it)
  \ in Frac: `drop` unconditionally would underflow, so check
  \ (there's no `depth` primitive yet — add one, or trust that typing
  \  def at the calculator prompt means they know what they're doing)

  \ --- simplest: expect a value, pop it into ax ---
  :ax
  \ now display:
  \ the prompt line shows "> <the input>"
  \ the result line shows ax in decimal
;
```

Wait — eval doesn't leave anything on the stack unless the compiled code pushes. And the user's expression does push. So after eval, the top of stack is the result. Good.

But :ax on an empty stack underflows silently (need(1) returns without popping). Not ideal, but acceptable for v1.

The main loop

```
def main
  \ reserve history is already zeroed (kernel BSS)
  0 :hp

  begin
    \ --- draw the whole screen ---
    0 clearfb                          \ or text-mode clear
    " FracCalc " type
    \ show accumulator
    "ans: " type  ax ~  "\n" type
    "> " type
    clbuf ln type

    \ --- read a key ---
    key  :sc

    \ Enter (0x1C)
    sc 28 - if0
      \ save to history
      hp chist + :hp2          \ (this line is pseudo — see notes)
      \ actually: chist slot = hp % hmax
      hp 1 + :hp               \ advance
      hist-save hp 1 - hmax %
      \ eval
      calculate
      \ clear line for next input
      0 :ln 0 :cx
    else
    \ Backspace (0x0E)
    sc 14 - if0
      bksp
    else
    \ Up (0x48)
    sc 72 - if0
      hp if0 else
        hp 1 - :hp
        hp hmax % hist-load
      then
    else
    \ Down (0x50)
    sc 80 - if0
      hp hmax 1 - - if0 else
        hp 1 + :hp
        hp hmax % hist-load
      then
    else
    \ Ctrl-L (0x26)
    sc 38 - if0
      \ clear current line and value
      0 :ln 0 :cx 0 :ax
    else
    \ Escape (0x01)
    sc 1 - if0
      exit
    else
    \ printable char: map and insert
    sc scancode->char  ins
    then then then then then then then
  again
;
```

Pseudo-code caveats aside, that's the whole program — 80 lines, and every line is Frac. The else-chaining is ugly (no case), but it works, and sc 28 - if0 … else … then is a clean idiom once you get used to it.

The twist that makes this a capstone

Because eval compiles through the same compiler that built the calculator, typing def at the calculator prompt is legal:

```
Frac> load calc.frac
FracCalc> ans: 0
> def twice dup + ;
> 
FracCalc> > 7 twice
FracCalc> ans: 14
```

That's self-extension in the calculator, using the same mechanism the OS uses. You're not editing a REPL — you're extending the language the calculator is written in. And the extension persists (it's in the dictionary), so the next expression can use it, and the next, and the next.

Now combine that with the history:

```
FracCalc> > 22/7
FracCalc> ans: 22/7
FracCalc> > 355/113  a -  abs
FracCalc> ans: 121/791
FracCalc> ↑↑↑                          (recall "22/7")
FracCalc> > 22/7  a  -  abs            (edit → re-evaluate against new ans)
FracCalc> ans: 0
```

The ↑ key recalls the last expression; you edit it (backspace, retype), press Enter, and the accumulator updates. That's exactly the TI-84 ans workflow, and it's now a Frac word.

Editing the program

Two levels:

Level 1 — cheap. At the calculator prompt, type def help "calc: type expressions, def to extend" type ;. Now help is a word. That's self-extension, and it's already a feature.

Level 2 — the deep one. Type edit at the prompt, and edit does:

```
def edit
  "calc.frac" "calc.frac.bak" copy          \ back up
  "calc.frac" line-editor                   \ open a text editor on the source
;
```

Where line-editor is a second program (or the same calculator, since it's also a Frac source file). You load the source as text into a buffer, edit it with the same line editor, save it, then load calc.frac reloads the (modified) calculator with the new behavior. Reboot optional — load recompiles everything and calls main again.

The whoa moment: you're inside the calculator, you type edit, you modify the calculator's own source, you save, you press Enter, and the calculator's UI changes underneath you. That's the TempleOS experience, in 200 lines of Frac source, on top of a compiler written in Frac, on top of a kernel written in Frac.

