What this demonstrates

Two things, and they're both fundamental.

First: the calculator is not a special application. It's a Frac program using key, eval, and def — exactly what a user has access to. Anything the calculator does, the user can write. That's the property Forth, Smalltalk, and TempleOS share and no modern OS does: the system is written in the same language it exposes to you.

Second: eval is the bridge between "calculator" and "self-hosted compiler." When you type eval at the calculator prompt, eval compiles a string. When you type load calc.frac, load compiles a file. When Stage 2 lands, eval becomes a Frac word that reads a token stream and emits machine code — the same thing eval already does, one level up. The calculator and the self-hosted compiler are the same shape. One compiles expression strings; the other compiles frac.frac itself. That's the arc, made concrete.

